{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "48",
    "case_summary": "A 78-year-old man was referred to our Institution due to the discovery of an anal mass during a routine physical examination. His medical history includes cancer of the right lung, which was treated with a right pneumonectomy one year prior. Routine blood tests and common neoplastic markers were within normal limits. A rectal examination revealed a well-defined mass on the left-anterior aspect of the anal canal, starting 1 cm from the anal verge and extending cranially for approximately 4 cm. Endoanal ultrasonography identified a 4 × 2 cm mass within the thickness of the sphincteric muscles. A total body CT scan confirmed the presence of the mass and showed no lymph node enlargement nearby or distant metastases. The mass appeared circumscribed and did not infiltrate the surrounding tissues.\n\nHistological examination showed a proliferation of densely packed spindle cells, with prominent nuclear palisading. Nuclear atypia was absent and mitotic count was of 4 mitosis/50HPF. Neoplastic cells showed diffuse and marked cytoplasmic positivity for KIT protein and CD34 in the majority of cells. Neoplastic cells were negative for desmin that stained residual smooth muscle fibers of bowel wall at the margins of the neoplasm.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
